504 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 112 0 R
/T (Code Explanation)
/E (Code Explanation)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
505 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 836 0 R
/E (In this Arduino program, a sound sensor is connected to analog pin A3 \(SOUND_PIN\) and a TM1637 4-digit display is connected to pins 8 \(CLK_PIN\) and 9 \(DIO_PIN\). The display shows the sound level in approximate decibels \(dB\).)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
506 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 837 0 R
/E (The setup\(\) function runs once when the Arduino starts. It begins serial communication using Serial.begin\(9600\), sets the display brightness using setBrightness\(\), and clears the display.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
507 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 838 0 R
/E (A separate function called readDb\(\) is used to calculate the sound level. It reads multiple samples from the sensor for a short time and finds the maximum and minimum values. The difference \(peak-to-peak value\) is converted into voltage.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
508 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 839 0 R
/E (Then, using a logarithmic formula, the voltage is converted into a dB value. A calibration offset is added, and the value is limited between 0 and 99.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
509 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 840 0 R
/E (The loop\(\) function runs continuously. It calls readDb\(\) to get the sound level, prints it on the Serial Monitor, and displays it on the TM1637 display.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
510 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 112 0 R
/E (So overall, the Arduino measures sound intensity and shows it as a dB value on the display.)
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
